.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int libusb_try_lock_events (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Attempt to acquire the event handling lock\&. This lock is used to ensure that only one thread is monitoring libusb event sources at any one time\&.

.PP
You only need to use this lock if you are developing an application which calls poll() or select() on libusb's file descriptors directly\&. If you stick to libusb's event handling loop functions (e\&.g\&. \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP) then you do not need to be concerned with this locking\&.

.PP
While holding this lock, you are trusted to actually be handling events\&. If you are no longer handling events, you must call \fBlibusb_unlock_events()\fP as soon as possible\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 if the lock was obtained successfully 

.PP
1 if the lock was not obtained (i\&.e\&. another thread holds the lock) \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_lock_events (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx) \-> events_lock)"

.PP
Acquire the event handling lock, blocking until successful acquisition if it is contended\&. This lock is used to ensure that only one thread is monitoring libusb event sources at any one time\&.

.PP
You only need to use this lock if you are developing an application which calls poll() or select() on libusb's file descriptors directly\&. If you stick to libusb's event handling loop functions (e\&.g\&. \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP) then you do not need to be concerned with this locking\&.

.PP
While holding this lock, you are trusted to actually be handling events\&. If you are no longer handling events, you must call \fBlibusb_unlock_events()\fP as soon as possible\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_unlock_events (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx) \-> events_lock)"

.PP
Release the lock previously acquired with \fBlibusb_try_lock_events()\fP or \fBlibusb_lock_events()\fP\&. Releasing this lock will wake up any threads blocked on \fBlibusb_wait_for_event()\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_event_handling_ok (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx) \-> events_lock)"

.PP
Determine if it is still OK for this thread to be doing event handling\&. Sometimes, libusb needs to temporarily pause all event handlers, and this is the function you should use before polling file descriptors to see if this is the case\&.

.PP
If this function instructs your thread to give up the events lock, you should just continue the usual logic that is documented in \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP\&. On the next iteration, your thread will fail to obtain the events lock, and will hence become an event waiter\&.

.PP
This function should be called while the events lock is held: you don't need to worry about the results of this function if your thread is not the current event handler\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 if event handling can start or continue 

.PP
0 if this thread must give up the events lock \fBMulti-threaded I/O: the full story\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_event_handler_active (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Determine if an active thread is handling events (i\&.e\&. if anyone is holding the event handling lock)\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 if a thread is handling events 

.PP
0 if there are no threads currently handling events \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_interrupt_event_handler (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Interrupt any active thread that is handling events\&. This is mainly useful for interrupting a dedicated event handling thread when an application wishes to call \fBlibusb_exit()\fP\&.

.PP
Since version 1\&.0\&.21, \fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x01000105

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_lock_event_waiters (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx) \-> event_waiters_lock)"

.PP
Acquire the event waiters lock\&. This lock is designed to be obtained under the situation where you want to be aware when events are completed, but some other thread is event handling so calling \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP is not allowed\&.

.PP
You then obtain this lock, re-check that another thread is still handling events, then call \fBlibusb_wait_for_event()\fP\&.

.PP
You only need to use this lock if you are developing an application which calls poll() or select() on libusb's file descriptors directly, \fBand\fP may potentially be handling events from 2 threads simultaneously\&. If you stick to libusb's event handling loop functions (e\&.g\&. \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP) then you do not need to be concerned with this locking\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_unlock_event_waiters (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx) \-> event_waiters_lock)"

.PP
Release the event waiters lock\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_wait_for_event (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, struct timeval * tv) \-> event_waiters_lock)"

.PP
Wait for another thread to signal completion of an event\&. Must be called with the event waiters lock held, see \fBlibusb_lock_event_waiters()\fP\&.

.PP
This function will block until any of the following conditions are met:
.IP "1." 4
The timeout expires
.IP "2." 4
A transfer completes
.IP "3." 4
A thread releases the event handling lock through \fBlibusb_unlock_events()\fP
.PP

.PP
Condition 1 is obvious\&. Condition 2 unblocks your thread \fIafter\fP the callback for the transfer has completed\&. Condition 3 is important because it means that the thread that was previously handling events is no longer doing so, so if any events are to complete, another thread needs to step up and start event handling\&.

.PP
This function releases the event waiters lock before putting your thread to sleep, and reacquires the lock as it is being woken up\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fItv\fP maximum timeout for this blocking function\&. A NULL value indicates unlimited timeout\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 after a transfer completes or another thread stops event handling 

.PP
1 if the timeout expired 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if timeval is invalid \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_handle_events_timeout_completed (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, struct timeval * tv, int * completed)"

.PP
Handle any pending events\&. libusb determines "pending events" by checking if any timeouts have expired and by checking the set of file descriptors for activity\&.

.PP
If a zero timeval is passed, this function will handle any already-pending events and then immediately return in non-blocking style\&.

.PP
If a non-zero timeval is passed and no events are currently pending, this function will block waiting for events to handle up until the specified timeout\&. If an event arrives or a signal is raised, this function will return early\&.

.PP
If the parameter completed is not NULL then \fIafter obtaining the event handling lock\fP this function will return immediately if the integer pointed to is not 0\&. This allows for race free waiting for the completion of a specific transfer\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fItv\fP the maximum time to block waiting for events, or an all zero timeval struct for non-blocking mode 
.br
\fIcompleted\fP pointer to completion integer to check, or NULL 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if timeval is invalid 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failure \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_handle_events_timeout (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, struct timeval * tv)"

.PP
Handle any pending events\&. Like \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP, but without the completed parameter, calling this function is equivalent to calling \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP with a NULL completed parameter\&.

.PP
This function is kept primarily for backwards compatibility\&. All new code should call \fBlibusb_handle_events_completed()\fP or \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP to avoid race conditions\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fItv\fP the maximum time to block waiting for events, or an all zero timeval struct for non-blocking mode 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, or a LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_handle_events (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Handle any pending events in blocking mode\&. There is currently a timeout hard-coded at 60 seconds but we plan to make it unlimited in future\&. For finer control over whether this function is blocking or non-blocking, or for control over the timeout, use \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP instead\&.

.PP
This function is kept primarily for backwards compatibility\&. All new code should call \fBlibusb_handle_events_completed()\fP or \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP to avoid race conditions\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, or a LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_handle_events_completed (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, int * completed)"

.PP
Handle any pending events in blocking mode\&. Like \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP, with the addition of a completed parameter to allow for race free waiting for the completion of a specific transfer\&.

.PP
See \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout_completed()\fP for details on the completed parameter\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fIcompleted\fP pointer to completion integer to check, or NULL 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success, or a LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_handle_events_locked (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, struct timeval * tv) \-> events_lock)"

.PP
Handle any pending events by polling file descriptors, without checking if any other threads are already doing so\&. Must be called with the event lock held, see \fBlibusb_lock_events()\fP\&.

.PP
This function is designed to be called under the situation where you have taken the event lock and are calling poll()/select() directly on libusb's file descriptors (as opposed to using \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP or similar)\&. You detect events on libusb's descriptors, so you then call this function with a zero timeout value (while still holding the event lock)\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fItv\fP the maximum time to block waiting for events, or zero for non-blocking mode 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if timeval is invalid 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failure \fBMulti-threaded applications and asynchronous I/O\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_pollfds_handle_timeouts (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Determines whether your application must apply special timing considerations when monitoring libusb's file descriptors\&. This function is only useful for applications which retrieve and poll libusb's file descriptors in their own main loop (\fBThe more advanced option\fP)\&.

.PP
Ordinarily, libusb's event handler needs to be called into at specific moments in time (in addition to times when there is activity on the file descriptor set)\&. The usual approach is to use \fBlibusb_get_next_timeout()\fP to learn about when the next timeout occurs, and to adjust your poll()/select() timeout accordingly so that you can make a call into the library at that time\&.

.PP
Some platforms supported by libusb do not come with this baggage - any events relevant to timing will be represented by activity on the file descriptor set, and \fBlibusb_get_next_timeout()\fP will always return 0\&. This function allows you to detect whether you are running on such a platform\&.

.PP
Since v1\&.0\&.5\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 if you must call into libusb at times determined by \fBlibusb_get_next_timeout()\fP, or 1 if all timeout events are handled internally or through regular activity on the file descriptors\&. \fBPolling libusb file descriptors for event handling\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_get_next_timeout (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, struct timeval * tv)"

.PP
Determine the next internal timeout that libusb needs to handle\&. You only need to use this function if you are calling poll() or select() or similar on libusb's file descriptors yourself - you do not need to use it if you are calling \fBlibusb_handle_events()\fP or a variant directly\&.

.PP
You should call this function in your main loop in order to determine how long to wait for select() or poll() to return results\&. libusb needs to be called into at this timeout, so you should use it as an upper bound on your select() or poll() call\&.

.PP
When the timeout has expired, call into \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout()\fP (perhaps in non-blocking mode) so that libusb can handle the timeout\&.

.PP
This function may return 1 (success) and an all-zero timeval\&. If this is the case, it indicates that libusb has a timeout that has already expired so you should call \fBlibusb_handle_events_timeout()\fP or similar immediately\&. A return code of 0 indicates that there are no pending timeouts\&.

.PP
On some platforms, this function will always returns 0 (no pending timeouts)\&. See \fBNotes on time-based events\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fItv\fP output location for a relative time against the current clock in which libusb must be called into in order to process timeout events 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 if there are no pending timeouts, 1 if a timeout was returned, or \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_OTHER\fP on failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_set_pollfd_notifiers (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx, \fBlibusb_pollfd_added_cb\fP added_cb, \fBlibusb_pollfd_removed_cb\fP removed_cb, void * user_data)"

.PP
Register notification functions for file descriptor additions/removals\&. These functions will be invoked for every new or removed file descriptor that libusb uses as an event source\&.

.PP
To remove notifiers, pass NULL values for the function pointers\&.

.PP
Note that file descriptors may have been added even before you register these notifiers (e\&.g\&. at \fBlibusb_init_context()\fP time)\&.

.PP
Additionally, note that the removal notifier may be called during \fBlibusb_exit()\fP (e\&.g\&. when it is closing file descriptors that were opened and added to the poll set at \fBlibusb_init_context()\fP time)\&. If you don't want this, remove the notifiers immediately before calling \fBlibusb_exit()\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.br
\fIadded_cb\fP pointer to function for addition notifications 
.br
\fIremoved_cb\fP pointer to function for removal notifications 
.br
\fIuser_data\fP User data to be passed back to callbacks (useful for passing context information) 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const struct \fBlibusb_pollfd\fP ** libusb_get_pollfds (\fBlibusb_context\fP * ctx)"

.PP
Retrieve a list of file descriptors that should be polled by your main loop as libusb event sources\&. The returned list is NULL-terminated and should be freed with \fBlibusb_free_pollfds()\fP when done\&. The actual list contents must not be touched\&.

.PP
As file descriptors are a Unix-specific concept, this function is not available on Windows and will always return NULL\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIctx\fP the context to operate on, or NULL for the default context 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
a NULL-terminated list of \fBlibusb_pollfd\fP structures 

.PP
NULL on error 

.PP
NULL on platforms where the functionality is not available 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void libusb_free_pollfds (const struct \fBlibusb_pollfd\fP ** pollfds)"

.PP
Free a list of \fBlibusb_pollfd\fP structures\&. This should be called for all pollfd lists allocated with \fBlibusb_get_pollfds()\fP\&.

.PP
Since version 1\&.0\&.20, \fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x01000104

.PP
It is legal to call this function with a NULL pollfd list\&. In this case, the function will simply do nothing\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpollfds\fP the list of \fBlibusb_pollfd\fP structures to free 
.RE
.PP

